---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
---
<Base
  title="Why I started LightPhotos — LightPhotos Blog"
  desc="A Lightroom license that stopped working, years of photos I couldn't open, and the afternoon I gave up and built the small editor I actually needed."
  active="blogs"
>
  <main class="lp-page">
    <article class="lp-article">
      <header class="lp-article-head">
        <p class="lp-article-meta"><a href="/blogs.html">Blogs</a> &middot; <time datetime="2026-06-23">June 23, 2026</time></p>
        <h1>Why I started LightPhotos</h1>
        <p class="lp-article-dek">
          A Lightroom license stopped working, years of photos I couldn't
          open from Apple Photos, and the afternoon I gave up and built the small editor I actually needed.
        </p>
        <p class="lp-article-byline">Andrew Yao &middot; 5 min read</p>
      </header>

      <aside class="lp-tldr">
        <p class="lp-tldr-label">TL;DR</p>
        <p>
          I paid for Lightroom 6, and after a macOS upgrade I couldn't install
          it again. I moved to Apple Photos, and a few years and a few upgrades
          later I lost years of photos stuck in a library the newer version
          wouldn't open. The free editors I tried, like Darktable, wanted me to
          learn a whole craft when I just wanted to send some pictures to my
          friends. So I wrote down the few things I actually needed and built
          the smallest editor that does them, with coding agents writing most
          of the code.
        </p>
      </aside>

      <h2>What I learned</h2>
      <ul class="lp-article-list">
        <li>
          <strong>Keep your photos as plain files.</strong> A backup only helps
          if something can still open it. Ordinary files on a drive can be
          opened by pretty much any app, now or ten years from now.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Buying software once doesn't mean you'll always be able to use it.</strong>
          A macOS upgrade stopped my Lightroom license working just as
          completely as cancelling a subscription would have, and nothing
          warned me first.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>More features made the free editors harder for me.</strong>
          They could do everything, and that's why I couldn't get started with
          them.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Write the list before the code.</strong> Writing down what
          the first version had to do is what let me keep saying no to
          everything else.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Nothing quite fit</h2>
      <p>
        I'm an amateur photographer, and I like taking pictures with my Sony
        full-frame camera. I shoot RAW, which means the camera saves the
        unprocessed sensor data, so most of my shots need a little work before
        I can share them.
      </p>

      <figure class="lp-figure">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 640 224" role="img" aria-label="Five options and why each one failed. Lightroom Classic: the installer stopped running after a macOS upgrade. Luminar AI: not the keyboard-first way I edit. Apple Photos: beautiful and fast, with one deal breaker I can't get past. Darktable and friends: everything I needed behind everything I didn't. A Lightroom subscription: ten dollars a month for something I open a few times a year. All five lead to building a small one myself.">
          <text class="lp-fig-label" x="0" y="12">WHAT I TRIED FIRST</text>

          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="0" y="24" width="190" height="24" rx="5"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="12" y="40">Lightroom 6</text>
          <circle class="lp-fig-bad" cx="204" cy="36" r="3"></circle>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="216" y="40">I own it. After a clean OS upgrade and I tried to reinstall lightroom 6, the installer does not run on the new OS.</text>

          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="0" y="54" width="190" height="24" rx="5"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="12" y="70">Luminar AI</text>
          <circle class="lp-fig-bad" cx="204" cy="66" r="3"></circle>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="216" y="70">Fine, but not quite the experience and performance I was looking for.</text>

          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="0" y="84" width="190" height="24" rx="5"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="12" y="100">Apple Photos</text>
          <circle class="lp-fig-bad" cx="204" cy="96" r="3"></circle>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="216" y="100">Beautiful, fast, and one deal breaker I can't get past.</text>

          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="0" y="114" width="190" height="24" rx="5"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="12" y="130">Darktable and friends</text>
          <circle class="lp-fig-bad" cx="204" cy="126" r="3"></circle>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="216" y="130">Everything I needed, behind everything I didn't.</text>

          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="0" y="144" width="190" height="24" rx="5"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="12" y="160">Lightroom subscription</text>
          <circle class="lp-fig-bad" cx="204" cy="156" r="3"></circle>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="216" y="160">$10 a month for something I open once in a while.</text>

          <line class="lp-fig-line" x1="95" y1="168" x2="95" y2="184"></line>
          <path class="lp-fig-arrow" d="M90 184 L100 184 L95 194 Z"></path>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box-accent" x="0" y="196" width="190" height="28" rx="5"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-t" x="95" y="215" text-anchor="middle">Build a small one myself</text>
        </svg>
      </figure>

      <p>
        I really do like Apple Photos. It looks great, it's fast, and it does
        nearly everything I wanted. If you use it and you're happy, I
        completely understand why.
      </p>
      <p>
        There's one deal breaker for me, though, and it's a big one. Your whole
        library lives in a single file, in a format only Apple Photos can read.
        I found out what that means the hard way. A newer version of macOS
        refused to open the Photos libraries on my older backups, and several
        years of pictures went with them. If you've ever lost photos to an
        upgrade, you know how sick that feels.
      </p>
      <p>
        I can't get past that. The features and the speed are fine, but all of
        it depends on a format I don't control, and I'm not willing to take
        that risk again.
      </p>

      <figure class="lp-figure">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 640 208" role="img" aria-label="Two paths for the same photos. Locked in a library: your photos become one library file and are gone after an upgrade. Kept as ordinary files: your photos stay ordinary files and you can open them in anything.">
          <text class="lp-fig-label" x="0" y="12">A BACKUP YOU CAN'T OPEN IS NOT A BACKUP</text>

          <rect class="lp-fig-panel" x="0" y="24" width="640" height="84" rx="8"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-t" x="16" y="46">Locked in a library</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="16" y="58" width="176" height="30" rx="5"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="104" y="78" text-anchor="middle">Your photos</text>
          <line class="lp-fig-line" x1="192" y1="73" x2="214" y2="73"></line>
          <path class="lp-fig-arrow" d="M214 68 L224 73 L214 78 Z"></path>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="224" y="58" width="176" height="30" rx="5"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="312" y="78" text-anchor="middle">One library file</text>
          <line class="lp-fig-line" x1="400" y1="73" x2="422" y2="73"></line>
          <path class="lp-fig-arrow" d="M422 68 L432 73 L422 78 Z"></path>
          <rect class="lp-fig-bad-stroke" x="432" y="58" width="176" height="30" rx="5"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="520" y="78" text-anchor="middle">Gone after an upgrade</text>

          <rect class="lp-fig-panel" x="0" y="116" width="640" height="84" rx="8"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-t" x="16" y="138">Kept as ordinary files</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="16" y="150" width="176" height="30" rx="5"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="104" y="170" text-anchor="middle">Your photos</text>
          <line class="lp-fig-line" x1="192" y1="165" x2="214" y2="165"></line>
          <path class="lp-fig-arrow" d="M214 160 L224 165 L214 170 Z"></path>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="224" y="150" width="176" height="30" rx="5"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="312" y="170" text-anchor="middle">Ordinary files</text>
          <line class="lp-fig-line" x1="400" y1="165" x2="422" y2="165"></line>
          <path class="lp-fig-arrow" d="M422 160 L432 165 L422 170 Z"></path>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box-accent" x="432" y="150" width="176" height="30" rx="5"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="520" y="170" text-anchor="middle">Open them in anything</text>
        </svg>
        <figcaption>
          The photos and the backup drive are the same in both cases. What
          changes is whether you need one company's app to open them.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <p>
        A single library doesn't fit how I keep things either. My main drive is
        for recent work, and older shoots move off to other drives. I don't
        want hundreds of gigabytes sitting on my laptop's main drive, locked
        inside a library I might not be able to open in a few years.
      </p>

      <h2>So I built one</h2>
      <p>
        That afternoon I sat down to write a basic version myself. I wasn't
        trying to clone Lightroom. I wanted the smallest thing that would get
        these photos to my friends, so I wrote the list down before I wrote
        any code.
      </p>
      <ol class="lp-article-list">
        <li>
          <strong>Open any folder and show the photos in it.</strong> My photos stay as ordinary files on whatever drive holds them, so I can always open them with something else.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Rate photos from 1 to 5,</strong> so I can go through a shoot
          quickly and mark the keepers.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Filter by rating,</strong> so I only see and work on the photos I like.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Adjust exposure, highlights, shadows and the other basics.</strong>
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Keep every adjustment non-destructive.</strong> The original
          file is never touched, so I can always undo an edit or start over.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Export selected photos to JPEG,</strong> ready to send.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Run on a Mac.</strong>
        </li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        That's all it had to do. Anything that wasn't on the list stayed out of
        the first version, and having it written down is what helped me keep
        saying no.
      </p>

      <h2>What else I'm trying to learn</h2>
      <p>
        I try to write very little of the code myself and let coding agents do
        most of it. That sounds simple, but doing it well is hard. An agent will
        happily hand you something that compiles and runs, but gives the wrong
        result some of the time or crashes in a case nobody tested. A photo
        editor shows those problems quickly. If a slider lags, or a folder of
        RAW files uses up all your memory, you notice right away.
      </p>
      <p>
        So LightPhotos is two projects for me at once. It's the photo editor I
        wanted, and it's how I'm figuring out what it takes to build software
        with agents while still caring about quality and speed. That's mostly
        what these posts are about.
      </p>

      <h2>What comes next</h2>
      <p>
        In the next post I'll walk through the big design choices behind
        LightPhotos and what I gave up for each one. If any of this sounds
        familiar, I'd really like to hear how you dealt with it.
      </p>




